    "summary": "Epic Lovelight suite and tight jams across three sets",
    "blurb": "The MIT show shines with a monster Turn On Your Lovelight jam, plus standout renditions of Easy Wind, The Other One > Cryptical Envelopment and Candyman. Jerry’s banter and incendiary solos keep the energy high while the rhythm section drives the set‑breaks. Good for anyone wanting an early‑70s peak Dead performance.",
    "review": "On May 7, 1970 at MIT’s Dupont Gymnasium the Grateful Dead delivered a relaxed yet adventurous three‑set marathon that still feels fresh decades later. The first acoustic‑leaning set rolls through classics like \"Candyman\" and \"New Speedway Boogie,\" while the second set builds into an unforgettable Turn On Your Lovelight suite – a sprawling jam that weaves in \"Good Lovin'\" and a soaring sax‑free crescendo. Jerry Garcia’s humor (“Eleven” joke) and his incendiary lead work, especially on \"Easy Wind\" and the The Other One > Cryptical Envelopment sequence, keep the band locked in, with Mickey Hart’s rhythmic cues adding extra propulsion during high‑energy moments.\n\nAudience recordings capture the vibe despite early‑70s tape hiss; SirMick’s cleanup of the best source (gd1970-05-07.aud.sirmick.32276.sbeok.flac16) makes the Lovelight medley surprisingly clear, letting listeners hear the band’s joyous interplay. While occasional mic chatter and minor drop‑outs remind you it’s a fan tape, the performance quality outweighs these flaws, making this show a solid recommendation for anyone hunting early Dead magic.",
